1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,200
In this tutorial, we'll explain how to configure Target Scope in Burp Suite and describe all

2
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,680
of the different purposes that Target Scope can be used for.

3
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:22,800
You use Target Scope to tell Burp what you're currently testing.

4
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:28,760
Burp can then make use of this in various places to control what happens and help to

5
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:31,280
improve your efficiency.

6
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:38,160
To configure Target Scope, go to the Target Tool and the Scope tab.

7
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:41,300
Scope is based on URLs.

8
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:47,380
By default, nothing is in Scope, and you add things to Scope by adding them to the Include

9
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:54,200
list.

10
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:59,720
If necessary, you can then exclude some items from Scope by adding them to the Exclude

11
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:02,720
list.

12
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:12,980
A URL will be in Scope if it matches something in the Include list and doesn't match anything

13
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,220
in the Exclude list.

14
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:20,940
Some common ways to configure Scope are that you can include a single domain, or a folder

15
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:28,500
or path within a domain, or you can configure multiple domains and paths together.

16
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:36,500
You can use Exclude rules for various purposes, such as excluding boring content from Scope,

17
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:42,920
or excluding functionality that is out of the scope for the test you're doing, or to exclude

18
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:48,860
fragile functionality that might break if you test it, or functionality that is liable to

19
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:53,920
break other activities such as killing your session, deleting your account, or things like

20
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:55,520
that.

21
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:04,100
By default, Target Scope works off simple URL prefixes, and this is suitable for most purposes.

22
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:11,540
If you enable Advanced Scope Control, you can then define scope rules based on each part of

23
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:13,440
the URL individually.

24
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:19,180
The protocol, the host or IP address, the port number, the file path, and you can use regular

25
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:21,940
expressions for each item.

26
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:27,300
This is useful for more complex cases, for example if you want to exclude URLs in different

27
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:33,700
folders where the file name matches a certain pattern.

28
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:39,460
In general, the easiest way to define scope is to browse the target application with Burps

29
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:46,140
Browser, and then use the context menu on the sitemap, tree or table to define the scope.

30
00:02:46,140 --> 00:02:52,440
You can use the context menu, select an item and say Add to Scope, and then you can select

31
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:57,220
items beneath that, use the context menu, and say Remove from Scope.

32
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:02,560
If you do this, then the scope configuration will be updated to reflect the items that you've

33
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,740
selected.

34
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:06,720
So what can you use Target Scope for?

35
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:11,880
Well, the first thing that happens is that when you do add an item to scope, Burps will

36
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:17,520
prompt you to ask whether the proxy should stop sending out of scope items to the proxy

37
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:20,340
history or to other burp tools.

38
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:25,960
This can help keep your data clean, reduce the size of project files, and reduce memory

39
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:26,960
usage.

40
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:35,140
In the sitemap, there is a view filter, so you can select to only show in scope items.

41
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:38,100
Everything else gets hidden from the view.

42
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:44,740
You can do the same in the proxy history.

43
00:03:44,740 --> 00:03:50,080
Burp proxy's interception rules can be based on the target scope.

44
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:57,400
You can define rules which will only intercept items if they're in target scope.

45
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:02,380
If you've configured a target scope and you go to perform a scan, Burp will automatically

46
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:10,060
pre-configure the URLs to scan based on your scope.

47
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:15,060
In your crawl options, you can configure whether out of scope resources should be fetched by

48
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:20,400
the embedded browser during the scan.

49
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:27,060
If you create a live task, you can configure the scope of the task based on the target suite

50
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:28,700
scope.

51
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:35,460
If you use Burp's search function, you can configure whether only in scope items should be included

52
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:39,540
in the search.

53
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:44,700
When you save a copy of your project, you can also configure whether to only include in scope

54
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:48,700
items.

55
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:54,700
In Burp Intruder, you can configure whether intruder should follow redirections and the

56
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:56,700
URLs that should be included in the redirection.

57
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:01,460
So you can configure whether to base redirections on what is in target scope.

58
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:06,660
And you can do the same thing in Burp Repeater as well.

59
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:11,640
If you use Burp's session handling rules, each session handling rule can be configured based

60
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:12,640
on your target scope.

61
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:20,960
And finally, if you go to project options and connections, you can configure whether all out

62
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:26,960
of scope requests should be dropped regardless of where they're coming from within Burp.

63
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:32,840
So that's how to configure target scope in Burp Suite and all of the different purposes that

64
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,180
target scope can be used for.

65
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:42,640
So that's how to configure target scope.

